Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-15-2000||10:14 AM||||I saw that the other day.  I'm not sure if you could even get it to fit in a Fiero.  Even if you could, I don't know how you'd be able to use your visors.<P>If you want a way to install a driving light switch that looks fairly factory, try what I did.  This only works if you don't have either the rear window defroster or the trunk release.  The switch is the driving light switch from my sisters '90 (?) Pontiac 6000000STE.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/new_driving_light_switch.JPG"> <BR>The picture is a little fuzzy, but you can see what I did.  (Now I just need to find some nice driving lights.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||08-15-2000||11:00 AM||||Good idea, Monkeyman.<P>My idea was similar only it used a Fiero rear defrost switch.  That switch has a light indicator in it that illuminates when the switch is activated.  That way, you could tell if the driving lights are on or off.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-15-2000||02:46 PM||||I just used this because I happened to have it lying around.  It looked like it would work and it matched.  I probably should have bought the other switch, though.||||reg||1
